                         "Manual Errata"
                         ---------------                          
                          
On Pg. 32, the second paragraph, sentence 3 should read:  
"If you want to fly the current mission from the new base, you'll 
have to manually move your F-16s' staring waypoint over to it."

Also on page 32, in the fifth paragraph, the first sentence 
should read: 
"On a similar note, if you ever have a problem getting
your F-16 onto a strip (you start out above the runway or in the 
center of the theater away from all runways), you can force 
StratFalc to fix the situation:  turn on the "Show ILSs"
option and then move your F-16 Flight 1, Waypoint 0 to any 
airbase that does not have an ILS.  Press "Save."  Now move 
that waypoint back to the strip that you want to start from 
and press "Save" again." 

Throughout the manual, where the "+" sign is used with respect
to flight numbers or waypoints, it normally means the "ñ" symbol.



           ---------------------------------------------
           "Troubleshooting & Tips for Strategic Falcon"
           ---------------------------------------------
 
Q.   I'm trying to fly a mission in Falcon 3.0 and it hangs up 
     before I can get into the cockpit. 
 
A.   This mainly occurs if you do NOT load STRTSPRT.COM prior to 
     running Falcon--or it might occur if you abandon a campaign 
     and don't run the "CLEAR.BAT" afterwards.  Just run the file 
     "CLEAR.BAT" from either your F-16 or Strategic Falcon 
     directory (type "CLEAR" <Enter>).  This will clear all the 
     read-only bits on the mission files and allow Falcon 3.0 to 
     create new ones.  Try to get into the cockpit again. 
 
 
Q.   All right, I've run "CLEAR.BAT" and it still doesn't work. 
     Now what? 
 
A.   It's time to restore your previous campaign files.  Switch 
     to your Falcon directory and do the following: 
      
     a.)  Type "DIR SQU*.* /OD" <Enter> 
 
     b.)  Note which CAM file is last on the list (It'll look     
          like "SQUAD0.CAM" or "SQUAD1.CAM").  The important 
          information is the number after the word "SQUAD." 
 
     c.)  Copy the file "RESTRCAM.BAT" (which is located in 
          your Strategic Falcon directory) into your Falcon3 
          directory.
     
     d.)  From your Falcon directory, type "RESTRCAM ? <Enter>"  
          where the "?" must be replaced by the squad number 
          you discovered in step b, above.  
 
     e.)  Your files have been restored to the previous mission. 
 
 
Q.   It still didn't work. 
 
A.   Sorry, your campaign is gone.  Little consolation to you, 
     but make *sure* STRTSPRT.COM is loaded when you're using 
     StratFalc with any version of Falcon after 3.01.1.  Either 
     load it from your AUTOEXEC.BAT or use "CALL LOADTSR.BAT" 
     command (qualify that with your path to Strategic Falcon, 
     e.g., "CALL C:\STRAT\LOADTSR.BAT") from the batch file 
     you use to run Falcon 3.0. 
 
 
Q.   That wasn't very nice.  I was on day twelve and I had the 
     Iraqis on the ropes. 
 
A.   I said I was sorry. 


Q.   I'm confused.  When exactly do I have to use ILSRESET? 
 
A.   Probably never.  You should only use ILSRESET if you begin  
     a new campaign and, on the first mission, you find your F-16  
     away from all airstrips, limping above the center of the  
     theater.  This indicates that the airstrip where Falcon 3.0  
     wanted to place you has had it's ILS removed by a previous run  
     of Strategic Falcon.  Just exit Falcon 3.0, run "ILSRESET," 
     and start Falcon 3.0 again.
 
     You can also fix this problem simply by running Strategic 
     Falcon and using the technique described in the manual to
     "force" an ILS onto the desired strip--this will make sure 
     there's an ILS at the airstrip closest to your F-16 
     Flight 1, Waypoint 0. 


Q.   The allied and enemy flight waypoints are always jumping 
     all over the place.  How come they don't stay where I put 
     them?  

A.   The data storage for these waypoints in the *.MIS file is 
     only one byte;  they weren't designed to be user-movable,
     hence their storage space was optimized.  Any waypoint has 
     to be at a location that's an even multiple of 1/256 of the 
     width of a theater (1.25 nm).  Strategic Falcon places the 
     waypoint at the nearest such spot to the right and down.


Q.   I've read through the documentation and reached the 
     conclusion that I want to return to Falcon version 3.01.1. 
     Am I correct in understanding that I do *not* need to load 
     the STRTSPRT.COM TSR if I do this? 
 
A.   Yes, you are correct:  if you use Falcon 3.01.1, you never 
     have to worry about loading the TSR again.  But MAKE SURE 
     you do NOT disable version-checking inside Strategic Falcon 
     if you use Falcon 3.01.1!  If you disable version checking, 
     you're in for a heap of trouble. (StratFalc has safety 
     features in place to prevent such trouble, but people can 
     be *extremely* resourceful when defeating safety features.)
 
 
Q.   I'm using Falcon 3.01.1 and I own the Falcon Commander 
     Utilities from Keyboard Commander Corps.  Your program 
     "FalcCalc" from this set of utilities only supports Falcon 
     versions 3.02 and later.  I want my reports back and I want 
     to be able to print the map! 
 
A.   For the reports, use FalcCalc version 2.51 (the 
     "postcardware" version available on quality BBSs everywhere, 
     as well as in CompuServe's FSFORUM lib 8).  For the maps, 
     you're going to have to get a little tricky. 
 
     Follow this procedure: 
 
     a.)  First, change to your FCU directory. 
      
     b.)  Then create a batch file called "MAP.BAT" that contains 
          only this line: 
      
                    FALCSPRT %1 0 C:\FALCON3\ 
      
          -Substitute your Falcon 3.01.1 directory for 
           "C:\FALCON3\".  DON'T FORGET THE BACKSLASH AT THE END! 
      
          -If your printer is dot-matrix 24-pin, leave the "0" as 
           it is. 
           
          -If your printer is laser, change the "0" to "1". 
           
          -If your printer is dot-matrix 9-pin, change the "0" to 
           "2". 
                
          -If you don't have a printer, change "0" to "3". 
      
     c.)  Start the batch file with the number of the squad you're 
          using (you can discover this using the technique from 
          the second question, above).  If it was 0, for example, 
          you'd type "MAP 0" <Enter>. 
 

Q.   What is "FalcCalc," anyway?

A.   FalcCalc is a utility for Falcon 3.0 that enhances
     campaign play.  It has several features, but the major 
     ones are:

 -It automatically calculates fuel burn for every F-16 on a
  mission, taking into account the jet's weight, armament, and
  drag, as well as the speed, altitudes, and distances of the
  waypoints.  (Version 1.0 of the software did only this;  the
  name "FalcCalc" is kind of a pun on "Fuel Calc."

 -It calculates waypoint timings for every flight/every waypoint,
  so you can plan a mission to hit a target with split-second
  precision (in theory, anyway:  Falcon 3.0 is a lot like real
  life--it tends to throw you a lot of curves once you're off the
  ground!).

 -It prints "Kneeboard Sheets" for each mission, which detail
  (a) every waypoint's distance, speed, and action, and
  time-on-target; (b) who's flying which planes, by callsign
  and name; and (c) location of potential bingo fields, relative
  to one of your preset waypoints.

 -It is an intelligence gathering tool.  It tells you all about
  enemy flights, where they're located and what they're carrying;
  it tells you about allied flights involved in your mission;  it
  breaks down every site in the theater and tells you its
  strength and occupation;  it tells you which sites are the victory
  sites and which are defeat sites.

 All of the above features are available in FalcCalc 2.51, which
 is "postcardware" and is available on better BBSs everywhere, as 
 well as in lib 8 of the CompuServe FSFORUM.  Unfortunately,
 version 2.51 *only* supports Falcon 3.01/3.01.1.  (You can sense
 the hook coming, can't you?)

 With version 3.0, FalcCalc improved the format of several of its
 reports, but besides just supporting the newer versions of Falcon
 (3.02 through 3.04), it added a map, which shows you all the
 intelligence information for the flights and the theater
 graphically, and is printable so you can add it to your knee
 board.

 As of version 3.0, FalcCalc went "to the show" (it's now
 commercial).  It's one of many utilities included in the
 Keyboard Commander Corps "Falcon Commander Utilities."
 The other utilities are an updated "F3Maps" the famous Red 
 Flag mission generator and much more; "ShowF3," which allows 
 you to capture screen shots from Falcon3 and manipulate them; 
 "F3 Requisition," a squadron and supply management utility;
 "KCCMenu," which runs your entire Falcon suite of utilities  
 (it can run your entire computer, as well!);  and 30 
 professional Red Flag missions.
 
